IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Safety, Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 1988 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- what the injured Commonwealth
-- employee meets in the Comcare scheme: whether I am an "employee" (s 5), whether what I suffered is an "injury" (ss 5A,
-- 5B, 6), whether Comcare is liable (s 14), notice and the claim (ss 53, 54), medical expenses and travel to treatment
-- (s 16), which weekly rate of incapacity compensation applies and when it stops (ss 19, 23), permanent impairment (ss 24
-- to 26), compensation on death (ss 17, 18), and reconsideration and review (ss 62, 64).
--
-- SEAT: the employee. "I was injured at work as a Commonwealth employee. Am I covered, and what compensation, incapacity
-- payments and medical costs do I get?" Comcare (or a licensee) is the determining authority; its determinations and
-- the judgments the Act hands it are encoded as what they mean for that person, conditionally.
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 82, compilation date 1 July 2026, includes amendments up to Act No. 17, 2025 (register id
-- C2026C00285), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A03668.txt.
--
-- FORMULAS LOST IN THE DEPOSIT. The formulas of ss 8(1), (2) (normal weekly earnings), 16(6)(c) (travel), 19(2), (2C),
-- (2D), (3) (the weekly amounts), 27(2) (non-economic loss) and 13AA(3) appear in the deposit as "the formula: where:"
-- with nothing between. This encoding does not supply them from memory: it answers which weekly rate applies and the
-- adjustment percentage, not the dollar amount, and leaves non-economic loss out. See NOTES.md finding 1.
--
-- INDEXED AMOUNTS ARE INPUTS. The $80,000 permanent impairment maximum (s 24(9), indexed by CPI under s 13) and the
-- $400,000 and $110 death amounts (s 17(3)-(5), indexed by the Wage Price Index under s 13AA) are asked for, as in force.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. Work for an employer other than the Commonwealth, a Commonwealth authority or a licensee answers `not
-- covered`. Sections 7 (listed diseases), 8 (normal weekly earnings), 15 (property), 19(2B)-(2D) (a week split at the
-- 45-week mark), 19(3A)-(14), 20, 21, 21A, 22 (superannuation, lump sums, hospital), 23A, 27, 29, 29A, 30, 31 (household
-- and attendant care, redemption), Part III (rehabilitation), Part IV (common law), ss 55 to 59, Part VI beyond ss 62 and
-- 64, Parts VII onwards; Defence service (s 4AA, the MRCA and the DRCA).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the individual's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Safety, Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 1988 -- the injured employee's questions`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    `an employer that is not the Commonwealth, a Commonwealth authority or a licensed corporation is under a State or Territory workers compensation law, which is not encoded (s 5)`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `ss 5, 5A, 5B, 6 -- am I covered, and is it an injury`

-- s 5(1): "employee means: (a) a person who is employed by the Commonwealth or by a Commonwealth authority, whether the
-- person is so employed under a law of the Commonwealth or of a Territory or under a contract of service or
-- apprenticeship; or (b) a person who is employed by a licensed corporation" (a corporation holding a Comcare licence
-- under Part VIII; a borrowed status, asked as a fact).
DECLARE `My employer` IS ONE OF
    `the Commonwealth, such as a department or agency`
    `a Commonwealth authority`
    `a licensed corporation (a self-insurer licensed under the Comcare scheme)`
    `another employer`

-- s 5A(1): "injury means: (a) a disease suffered by an employee; or (b) an injury (other than a disease) suffered by an
-- employee, that is a physical or mental injury arising out of, or in the course of, the employee's employment; or (c) an
-- aggravation of a physical or mental injury (other than a disease) ... that arose out of, or in the course of, that
-- employment; but does not include a disease, injury or aggravation suffered as a result of reasonable administrative
-- action taken in a reasonable manner in respect of the employee's employment" (performance appraisal, counselling,
-- suspension, discipline, a failure to get a promotion or transfer: (2)).
-- s 5B: "disease means: (a) an ailment ...; or (b) an aggravation of such an ailment; that was contributed to, to a
-- significant degree, by the employee's employment"; "(3) significant degree means a degree that is substantially more
-- than material" (a judgment, asked as a fact).
DECLARE `What I suffered` IS ONE OF
    `an ailment or disease, or its aggravation, including a psychological condition that developed over time`
    `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one`

-- s 6(1): an injury "shall ... be treated as having so arisen if it was sustained: (a) as a result of an act of violence
-- that would not have occurred but for the employee's employment ...; (b) while the employee was at the employee's place
-- of work, for the purposes of that employment, or was temporarily absent from that place during an ordinary recess ...;
-- (c) while the employee was temporarily absent from the employee's place of work undertaking an activity ... associated
-- with the employee's employment; or ... at the direction or request of the Commonwealth or a licensee; or (d) while the
-- employee was, at the direction or request of the Commonwealth or a licensee, travelling for the purpose of that
-- employment" -- and (e) to (i) (education, medical certificates and treatment, overseas). (1C): "travel between the
-- employee's residence and the employee's usual place of work is taken not to be at the direction or request". (3): (1)
-- does not apply at a place or in a recess "if the employee sustained the injury because he or she voluntarily and
-- unreasonably submitted to an abnormal risk of injury".
DECLARE `Where I was hurt` IS ONE OF
    `at my workplace for work, or on an ordinary break from it`
    `away from my workplace doing something for work, or at my employer's direction or request`
    `travelling for work at my employer's direction or request`
    `by an act of violence that would not have happened but for my job`
    `at a place, or travelling, for education, a medical certificate, treatment or rehabilitation under s 6(1)(e) to (g)`
    `travelling between home and my usual workplace`
    `somewhere else`

GIVEN `where I was hurt` IS A `Where I was hurt`
      `I voluntarily and unreasonably submitted to an abnormal risk` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6 treats it as arising out of or in the course of employment` IF
    CONSIDER `where I was hurt`
    WHEN `travelling between home and my usual workplace` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `somewhere else` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `at my workplace for work, or on an ordinary break from it` THEN (NOT `I voluntarily and unreasonably submitted to an abnormal risk`)
    WHEN `at a place, or travelling, for education, a medical certificate, treatment or rehabilitation under s 6(1)(e) to (g)` THEN (NOT `I voluntarily and unreasonably submitted to an abnormal risk`)
    WHEN `away from my workplace doing something for work, or at my employer's direction or request` THEN (NOT `I voluntarily and unreasonably submitted to an abnormal risk`)
    OTHERWISE TRUE

DECLARE `Is it an injury` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is an "injury" under s 5A`
    `no: a disease must have been contributed to, to a significant degree (substantially more than material), by my employment (s 5B)`
    `no: it did not arise out of, or in the course of, my employment (ss 5A(1)(b), (c), 6)`
    `no: it resulted from reasonable administrative action taken in a reasonable manner (s 5A(1), (2))`

@export Is what happened to me an "injury" the Comcare scheme covers (ss 5, 5A, 5B, 6)?
GIVEN `my employer` IS A `My employer` @desc Who employed you when it happened
      `what I suffered` IS A `What I suffered` @desc Whether it is a disease or ailment (including one that developed over time, or its aggravation), or a physical or mental injury other than a disease (or its aggravation)
      `my work contributed to it to a significant degree` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a disease: whether your employment contributed to it to a significant degree, meaning substantially more than materially (Comcare's judgment, on the length and nature of your work, any predisposition and your life outside work)
      `where I was hurt` IS A `Where I was hurt` @desc For an injury other than a disease: where or how it happened
      `it otherwise arose out of or in the course of my work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an injury other than a disease: whether it arose out of, or in the course of, your employment in some other way (true if you are sure; the places listed in s 6 settle it without this)
      `I voluntarily and unreasonably submitted to an abnormal risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were hurt because you voluntarily and unreasonably exposed yourself to an abnormal risk of injury (for example a dangerous stunt during a break)
      `it resulted from reasonable administrative action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it resulted from reasonable administrative action taken in a reasonable manner, such as a reasonable performance appraisal, counselling, suspension, disciplinary action, or a decision about a promotion or transfer
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Is it an injury`
`Is it an injury under the Act` MEANS
    IF `my employer` EQUALS `another employer`
    THEN `not covered` `an employer that is not the Commonwealth, a Commonwealth authority or a licensed corporation is under a State or Territory workers compensation law, which is not encoded (s 5)`
    ELSE IF `it resulted from reasonable administrative action`
         THEN `answered` `no: it resulted from reasonable administrative action taken in a reasonable manner (s 5A(1), (2))`
         ELSE IF `what I suffered` EQUALS `an ailment or disease, or its aggravation, including a psychological condition that developed over time`
              THEN IF `my work contributed to it to a significant degree`
                   THEN `answered` `yes: it is an "injury" under s 5A`
                   ELSE `answered` `no: a disease must have been contributed to, to a significant degree (substantially more than material), by my employment (s 5B)`
              ELSE IF     `s 6 treats it as arising out of or in the course of employment` `where I was hurt` `I voluntarily and unreasonably submitted to an abnormal risk`
                      OR  `it otherwise arose out of or in the course of my work`
                   THEN `answered` `yes: it is an "injury" under s 5A`
                   ELSE `answered` `no: it did not arise out of, or in the course of, my employment (ss 5A(1)(b), (c), 6)`

§§ `s 14 -- is Comcare liable to compensate me`

-- s 14(1): "Comcare is liable to pay compensation ... in respect of an injury suffered by an employee if the injury results
-- in death, incapacity for work, or impairment." (2): "not payable in respect of an injury that is intentionally self-
-- inflicted." (3): "not payable in respect of an injury that is caused by the serious and wilful misconduct of the employee
-- but is not intentionally self-inflicted, unless the injury results in death, or serious and permanent impairment." s
-- 16(2): medical expenses are payable "whether or not the injury results in death, incapacity for work, or impairment".
DECLARE `Comcare's liability` IS ONE OF
    `yes: Comcare is liable to compensate me for the injury (s 14(1)), and for reasonable medical treatment (s 16)`
    `only medical expenses: the injury has not resulted in death, incapacity for work or impairment, but reasonable medical treatment is still compensated (ss 14(1), 16(2))`
    `no: an intentionally self-inflicted injury is not compensated (s 14(2))`
    `no: an injury caused by my serious and wilful misconduct is not compensated unless it results in death or serious and permanent impairment (s 14(3))`

@export Is Comcare liable to pay me compensation for my injury (ss 14, 16(2))?
GIVEN `it has caused incapacity for work, impairment or death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury has resulted in incapacity for work, an impairment, or death
      `it was intentionally self-inflicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury was intentionally self-inflicted
      `it was caused by my serious and wilful misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury was caused by your own serious and wilful misconduct (for example being drunk at work against instructions)
      `it caused death or serious and permanent impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury resulted in death or in a serious and permanent impairment
GIVETH A `Comcare's liability`
`Is Comcare liable` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `it was intentionally self-inflicted` THEN `no: an intentionally self-inflicted injury is not compensated (s 14(2))`
        IF `it was caused by my serious and wilful misconduct` AND (NOT `it caused death or serious and permanent impairment`)
            THEN `no: an injury caused by my serious and wilful misconduct is not compensated unless it results in death or serious and permanent impairment (s 14(3))`
        IF `it has caused incapacity for work, impairment or death` THEN `yes: Comcare is liable to compensate me for the injury (s 14(1)), and for reasonable medical treatment (s 16)`
        OTHERWISE `only medical expenses: the injury has not resulted in death, incapacity for work or impairment, but reasonable medical treatment is still compensated (ss 14(1), 16(2))`

§§ `ss 53, 54 -- notice and the claim`

-- s 53(1): "This Act does not apply in relation to an injury to an employee unless notice in writing of the injury is
-- given to the relevant authority: (a) as soon as practicable after the employee becomes aware of the injury". (3): a late
-- or defective notice is taken to be given if the authority would not be prejudiced, or the failure was from death,
-- absence from Australia, ignorance, mistake "or any other reasonable cause".
-- s 54(1): "Compensation is not payable ... unless a claim for compensation is made". (2): "(a) a written claim in
-- accordance with the form approved by Comcare ...; and (b) except where the claim is for compensation under section 16
-- or 17--a certificate by a legally qualified medical practitioner". (3): without the certificate, the claim is "taken
-- not to have been made until such a certificate is given". (5): "substantial compliance is sufficient".
DECLARE `Kind of claim` IS ONE OF
    `medical expenses only (s 16)`
    `compensation on a death (s 17)`
    `weekly incapacity, permanent impairment or another kind of compensation`

@export Have I given notice and made a claim that counts (ss 53, 54)?
GIVEN `I gave written notice of the injury as soon as practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave your employer or Comcare written notice of the injury as soon as practicable after becoming aware of it
      `a late or defective notice is excused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If not: whether the authority would not be prejudiced by treating it as enough, or the failure came from absence from Australia, ignorance, a mistake or another reasonable cause
      `I made a written claim substantially in the approved form` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave a written claim substantially in the form Comcare approved
      `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What the claim is for
      `I gave a medical practitioner's certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave a certificate from a medical practitioner on the approved form
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I made a claim that counts` IF
        (`I gave written notice of the injury as soon as practicable` OR `a late or defective notice is excused`)
    AND `I made a written claim substantially in the approved form`
    AND (    `I gave a medical practitioner's certificate`
         OR  `the kind of claim` EQUALS `medical expenses only (s 16)`
         OR  `the kind of claim` EQUALS `compensation on a death (s 17)`)

§§ `s 16 -- medical expenses and travel to treatment`

-- s 16(1): Comcare is liable for "the cost of medical treatment obtained in relation to the injury (being treatment that it
-- was reasonable for the employee to obtain in the circumstances)", of "such amount as Comcare determines is appropriate".
-- (6): for a necessary journey to obtain that treatment, and for staying there, at a rate per kilometre the Minister
-- specifies (the formula is lost in the deposit; the rate is not deposited). (7): "not liable ... unless: (a) the
-- reasonable length of such a journey ... (including the return part of the journey) exceeded 50 kilometres; or (b) if the
-- journey ... involved the use of public transport or ambulance services--the employee's injury reasonably required the
-- use of such transport or services regardless of the distance involved."
@export Can I claim the cost of travelling to medical treatment for my injury (s 16(6), (7))?
GIVEN `the treatment is compensable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether Comcare is paying (or must pay) for the medical treatment you travelled to
      `the reasonable return distance in kilometres` IS A NUMBER @desc The reasonable length of the necessary journey, there and back, in kilometres
      `I used public transport or an ambulance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the journey used public transport or an ambulance
      `my injury reasonably required it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your injury reasonably required public transport or an ambulance (ignored if you used neither)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I claim travel to treatment` IF
        `the treatment is compensable`
    AND (    `the reasonable return distance in kilometres` GREATER THAN 50
         OR  (`I used public transport or an ambulance` AND `my injury reasonably required it`))

§§ `ss 19, 23 -- weekly compensation for incapacity` -- arises only if the person cannot work, or work as before

-- s 19(2), (2A): for "each week that is a maximum rate compensation week" -- a week in which the incapacity prevents my
-- working my normal weekly hours or at my pre-injury level, while "the total number of hours that the employee has been
-- prevented from working ... in that week and in all previous weeks ... does not exceed 45 times the employee's normal
-- weekly hours" -- the compensation is worked out from NWE (normal weekly earnings) and AE (the greater of what I am able
-- to earn in suitable employment and what I earn). (3): for every other week of incapacity, from an "adjustment percentage"
-- applied to NWE: "(a) if the employee is not employed during that week--75%; or (b) ... employed for 25% or less of his or
-- her normal weekly hours ...--80%; or (c) ... more than 25% but not more than 50% ...--85%; or (d) ... more than 50% but
-- not more than 75% ...--90%; or (e) ... more than 75% but less than 100% ...--95%; or (f) ... 100% ...--100%."
-- s 23(1): "Compensation is not payable under section 19 ... to an employee who has reached pension age." (1A): if injured
-- at or after "the age that is 2 years before pension age", it is payable "for a maximum of 104 weeks (whether consecutive
-- or not)". (2): not while imprisoned for an offence. Pension age is the Social Security Act's (a borrowed term, asked).
GIVEN `the hours I was employed this week` IS A NUMBER
      `my normal weekly hours` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the adjustment percentage` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the hours I was employed this week` AT MOST 0 THEN 75
        IF `the share` AT MOST 0.25 THEN 80
        IF `the share` AT MOST 0.5 THEN 85
        IF `the share` AT MOST 0.75 THEN 90
        IF `the share` LESS THAN 1 THEN 95
        OTHERWISE 100
    WHERE
        `the share` MEANS `the hours I was employed this week` DIVIDED BY `my normal weekly hours`

DECLARE `Weekly compensation` IS ONE OF
    `a maximum rate compensation week: compensation is worked out on all of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(2))`
    `a reduced rate week: compensation is worked out on this percentage of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(3))` HAS `adjustment percentage` IS A NUMBER
    `none: I have reached pension age (s 23(1))`
    `none: the 104 weeks for an injury suffered within 2 years before pension age are used up (s 23(1A))`
    `none: I am imprisoned for an offence (s 23(2))`
    `none: I am not incapacitated for work this week`

@export Which rate of weekly incapacity compensation applies to me this week (ss 19(2), (2A), (3), 23)?
GIVEN `I am incapacitated for work this week` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of the injury, you cannot work your normal weekly hours this week, or cannot work at your level before the injury
      `my normal weekly hours` IS A NUMBER @desc The hours you normally worked each week before the injury
      `the hours I have been prevented from working, to the end of this week` IS A NUMBER @desc The total hours of your normal weekly hours that the incapacity has prevented you from working (or working at your old level), in this week and every earlier week of incapacity
      `the hours I was employed this week` IS A NUMBER @desc The hours you were employed this week, in any job (0 if none)
      `I have reached pension age` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reached pension age under the Social Security Act 1991 (67 for most people)
      `I was injured within 2 years before pension age` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were injured at or after the age 2 years before your pension age
      `the weeks of incapacity already compensated` IS A NUMBER @desc How many weeks of incapacity have already been compensated for this injury (counted only for an injury within 2 years before pension age)
      `I am imprisoned for an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are in prison this week in connection with your conviction of an offence
GIVETH A `Weekly compensation`
`Which weekly rate applies` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `I am incapacitated for work this week`) THEN `none: I am not incapacitated for work this week`
        IF `I am imprisoned for an offence` THEN `none: I am imprisoned for an offence (s 23(2))`
        IF `I was injured within 2 years before pension age` AND `the weeks of incapacity already compensated` AT LEAST 104
            THEN `none: the 104 weeks for an injury suffered within 2 years before pension age are used up (s 23(1A))`
        IF `I have reached pension age` AND (NOT `I was injured within 2 years before pension age`)
            THEN `none: I have reached pension age (s 23(1))`
        IF `the hours I have been prevented from working, to the end of this week` AT MOST (45 TIMES `my normal weekly hours`)
            THEN `a maximum rate compensation week: compensation is worked out on all of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(2))`
        OTHERWISE `a reduced rate week: compensation is worked out on this percentage of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(3))` (`the adjustment percentage` `the hours I was employed this week` `my normal weekly hours`)

§§ `ss 24 to 26 -- permanent impairment` -- arises only if the injury has left a permanent impairment

-- s 24(3), (4): the amount is "the same percentage of the maximum amount as the percentage determined by Comcare under
-- subsection (5)" (the degree of permanent impairment under the approved Guide, a judgment, asked as a figure), "not
-- exceeding the maximum amount at the date of the assessment". (9): "the maximum amount is $80,000", indexed under s 13
-- (an input). (7): nothing is payable for an impairment other than a hearing loss if the degree "is less than 10%";
-- (7A): for a hearing loss, if "the binaural hearing loss ... is less than 5%". (8): (7) does not apply to the loss, or
-- loss of use, of a finger or a toe, or the loss of the sense of taste or smell.
DECLARE `Kind of impairment` IS ONE OF
    `a hearing loss`
    `the loss, or loss of use, of a finger or toe, or the loss of the sense of taste or smell`
    `another impairment`

@export How much permanent impairment compensation will I get (s 24)?
GIVEN `the kind of impairment` IS A `Kind of impairment` @desc What kind of permanent impairment it is
      `the degree of permanent impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc The degree of permanent impairment Comcare determines under the approved Guide, as a percentage (for example 12 for 12%)
      `the binaural hearing loss` IS A NUMBER @desc For a hearing loss: your binaural hearing loss as Comcare determines it, as a percentage (ignored otherwise)
      `the maximum amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The s 24 maximum amount in force at the date of assessment, in dollars ($80,000 in the Act, indexed each 1 July by the CPI under s 13)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much permanent impairment compensation` MEANS
    IF `below the threshold`
    THEN 0
    ELSE min `the maximum amount` (`the maximum amount` TIMES `the degree of permanent impairment` DIVIDED BY 100)
    WHERE
        `below the threshold` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the kind of impairment`
            WHEN `a hearing loss` THEN `the binaural hearing loss` LESS THAN 5
            WHEN `another impairment` THEN `the degree of permanent impairment` LESS THAN 10
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 25(4): after a final assessment (other than a hearing loss), "no further amounts of compensation shall be payable ...
-- in respect of a subsequent increase in the degree of impairment, unless the increase is 10% or more"; (5): for a hearing
-- loss, "unless the subsequent increase in the degree of binaural hearing loss is 5% or more".
@export My impairment has got worse since Comcare's final assessment: can I get more permanent impairment compensation (s 25(4), (5))?
GIVEN `it is a hearing loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the impairment is a hearing loss
      `the increase in percentage points` IS A NUMBER @desc How much the degree of permanent impairment (or, for a hearing loss, the binaural hearing loss) has increased since the final assessment, in percentage points
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I get more for a worse impairment` IF
    IF `it is a hearing loss`
    THEN `the increase in percentage points` AT LEAST 5
    ELSE `the increase in percentage points` AT LEAST 10

-- s 26(1): "an amount of compensation payable to an employee under section 24 or 25, shall be paid to the employee within
-- 30 days after the date of the assessment"; (2): if not, interest runs from the end of those 30 days (at a rate the
-- Minister specifies); (4): not while a reconsideration or review of the determination is under way.
@export By what day must my permanent impairment compensation be paid, before interest runs (s 26)?
GIVEN `the date of the assessment` IS A DATE @desc The date Comcare assessed the amount of your permanent impairment compensation
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must my impairment compensation be paid` MEANS `the date of the assessment` PLUS 30

§§ `ss 17, 18 -- if the employee dies` -- arises only if the injury results in death; asked by a dependant

-- s 17(2): "if the employee dies without leaving dependants, compensation is not payable" (apart from ss 16 and 18). (3):
-- if some dependants were wholly dependent, "Comcare is liable to pay compensation ... of $400,000" for all of them. (4):
-- if only partly dependent dependants, "such amount, not exceeding $400,000, as Comcare determines, having regard to any
-- losses suffered". (5): $110 a week for each prescribed child wholly or mainly dependent. s 13AA indexes these by the Wage
-- Price Index (inputs). s 18: reasonable funeral costs up to $9,000 (indexed under s 13), or a higher prescribed amount.
-- s 4 "dependant": the spouse, a parent, step-parent, parent-in-law, grandparent, child, stepchild, grandchild, sibling or
-- half-sibling, or a person in a parent-child relationship, "wholly or partly dependent on the employee at the date of the
-- employee's death".
DECLARE `Lump sum on death` IS ONE OF
    `the full lump sum, for all the dependants, shared as Comcare determines (s 17(3), (8))` HAS `amount` IS A NUMBER
    `an amount Comcare determines, up to this, for the partly dependent dependants (s 17(4))` HAS `at most` IS A NUMBER
    `no lump sum: the employee left no dependants (s 17(2)); medical and funeral costs are still payable (ss 16, 18)`

@export My family member died of a work injury: what lump sum is payable to the dependants (s 17(2) to (4))?
GIVEN `some dependants were wholly dependent on the employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any dependant (spouse, parent, child, sibling and so on) was wholly dependent on the employee for economic support at the date of death
      `some dependants were partly dependent on the employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any dependant was partly dependent on the employee at that date
      `the s 17 lump sum in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The s 17(3) amount in force, in dollars ($400,000 in the Act, indexed each 1 July by the Wage Price Index under s 13AA)
GIVETH A `Lump sum on death`
`What lump sum is payable on death` MEANS
    IF `some dependants were wholly dependent on the employee`
    THEN `the full lump sum, for all the dependants, shared as Comcare determines (s 17(3), (8))` `the s 17 lump sum in force`
    ELSE IF `some dependants were partly dependent on the employee`
         THEN `an amount Comcare determines, up to this, for the partly dependent dependants (s 17(4))` `the s 17 lump sum in force`
         ELSE `no lump sum: the employee left no dependants (s 17(2)); medical and funeral costs are still payable (ss 16, 18)`

§§ `ss 62, 64 -- reconsideration and review`

-- s 61(1)(c): the notice of a determination tells me I may "request a reconsideration of the determination under
-- subsection 62(2)". s 62(3): the request must "(a) set out the reasons for the request; and (b) be given to the
-- determining authority within 30 days after the day on which the determination first came to the notice of the person
-- making the request, or within such further period ... as the determining authority ... allows". s 60(1): "reviewable
-- decision means a decision made under subsection 38(4) or section 62". s 64(1), (3): the Administrative Review Tribunal
-- reviews only a reviewable decision, so the reconsideration comes first.
@export By what day must I ask Comcare to reconsider its determination on my claim (s 62(3))?
GIVEN `the day the determination first came to my notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you first learned of the determination (usually the day you received the notice)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I ask for reconsideration` MEANS `the day the determination first came to my notice` PLUS 30

DECLARE `Review` IS ONE OF
    `first ask the determining authority to reconsider it, within 30 days (s 62); only the decision on reconsideration can go to the Tribunal (ss 60(1), 64)`
    `I may apply to the Administrative Review Tribunal for review of the reconsideration decision (s 64(1)(a))`

@export Can I take Comcare's decision on my claim to the Administrative Review Tribunal (ss 60, 62, 64)?
GIVEN `the determination has been reconsidered under s 62` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the determination has been reconsidered under s 62 (on your request or Comcare's own motion) and a decision made affirming, varying or revoking it
GIVETH A `Review`
`Can I go to the Tribunal` MEANS
    IF `the determination has been reconsidered under s 62`
    THEN `I may apply to the Administrative Review Tribunal for review of the reconsideration decision (s 64(1)(a))`
    ELSE `first ask the determining authority to reconsider it, within 30 days (s 62); only the decision on reconsideration can go to the Tribunal (ss 60(1), 64)`
